:undisputed:history:
= truth conditional semantics =
====== short ======
Semantic theories based upon the claim that meanings are truth conditions.
now aka *possible worlds semantics*

====== related to ======
hub: [[influential_semantic_theories]]
++[[fregean_semantics]]
++[[russellian_semantics]]

= main =
The original naive version of this is sometimes expressed by early analytic
philosophers (such as Russell) as the doctrine that the meaning of a sentence is
its truth conditions. Or, to remain agnostic about the existence of meanings,
sentences are _synonymous_ iff they are true under the same conditions (or in
the same possible worlds).

Consideration of indexicals and the realisation that the truth conditions of
some sentences may vary depending upon their contexts of assertion and
evaluation led to refinement and increased sophistication.

On the modern view:
Assertions (i.e. sentences asserted in a given context of assertion) are
_synonymous_ iff they have the same truth-value with respect to every
circumstance of evaluation.

Or as the [[https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2025/entries/meaning/|Stanford Encyclopedia]] somewhat tendentiously puts it:
    two sentences express the same proposition if and only if it is impossible
    for them to differ in truth-value.
(this is a modern version of [[fregean_semantics#Freges criterion of synonymy]]
hence that can be taken as a form of truth functional semantics - however senses
complicate things)

A function from possible worlds to truth values is sometimes called an
intension in more recent (quasi-technical) literature.

=== objections ===

The division into the fixation of truth conditions is not fine enough.
==== all necessary truths synonymous ====
It seems implausible that all necessary truths assert the same proposition
despite being true in the same possible worlds (i.e. all of them).

e.g. It seems that
    1)    2 + 2 = 4
    2)    There are infinitely many prime numbers
have different meanings despite both being true in every possible world (and
hence true in exactly the same possible worlds).

==== hyperintensionality ====
More generally([[#note1]]), it seems that two statements may have the same truth conditions
(and hence necessarily have the same truth value) and yet a person believe just
one of them. But if a person believes just one of them then, it is suggested,
they have different meanings.

Or, two predicates two (n-place) predicates are necessarily true of the same
(tuples/)objects and yet may be viewed as expressing different concepts.

This can be viewed as what happens if you strip the “rational agent” qualifier
out of [[fregean_semantics#Freges criterion of synonymy]]. If you assume that
the fact that someone believes(/could judge) just one of two statements
to be true, shows that they are not synonymous even if the person is irrational
(or at least not perfectly rational/logically omniscient) or doesn't "fully"
understand them, or has not reflected on them, holds just one of them true.

*note1* Actually this may not be more general. For if statements _P_ and _Q_
necessarily have the same truth value, then ⌜ _P_ iff _Q_ ⌝(:The result of putting the sentence _P_ before "iff" and the sentence _Q_ after.:) is necessarily true.

This makes it salient that [[fregean_semantics#Freges criterion of synonymy]]
relies upon logical omniscience.

===== history =====
While this objection is best known from
[[local:zz.texts/cresswell1974-hyperintensionalLogic.pdf]] <- Suszko 1973, “Abolition of the Fregean Axiom”
and
Jago 2009, “Logical Information and Epistemic Space” doi:10.1007/s11229-008-9411-x
Jago 2014, “The Impossible: An Essay on Hyperintensionality” doi:10.1093/acprof:oso/9780198709008.001.0001

it is there Right from the start:
	The usual definition given there is: 'A right angle is an angle equal to its
	adjacent angle.' The author [Husserl] would probably comment on this as
	follows : 'The idea (Vorstellung) of a right angle is a simple one; so it is
	a wholly mistaken procedure to try to define it. Relation to another,
	adjacent, angle is in no way involved in our idea of a right angle. It is
	indeed correct to say: The concepts "right angle" and "angle equal to its
	adjacent angle" have the same extension; but it is not correct to say they
	have the same content. The extension is being defined, in place of the
	content. If the definition were correct, any assertion that some- thing is a
	right angle would refer, not to the concrete pair of sides we have before
	us, but only to their relation to another pair.  All that we can admit is
	that equality with the adjacent angle gives us a necessary and sufficient
	criterion for a right angle.'

==== propositional attitudes ====
[[propositional_attitudes]]
If sentence meanings are also the objects of belief (i.e. they are both
propositions) and the sentences (1) and (2) are synonymous, then the belief
ascriptions:
    Mary believes that 2 + 2 = 4
    Mary believes that there are infinitely many prime numbers
should also be synonymous. But it seems possible for them to differ
in truth value and hence they are _not_ synonymous (according to truth
conditional semantics itself).
(but see [[fregean_semantics#unknown synonyms]] for an attempted defence)
